Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(x^{2} y^{3}\right)&=3 x^{2} y^{2} y' + 2 x y^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2 x y^{3} + y' \left(3 x^{2} y^{2} + 1\right)&=6 x^{2}\\
\left(3 x^{2} y^{2} + 1\right)y^{\prime}&=6 x^{2} - 2 x y^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{6 x^{2} - 2 x y^{3}}{3 x^{2} y^{2} + 1}
$$

This formula applies where $3 x^{2} y^{2} + 1\ne0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3 x^{2} \sin{\left(y \right)}\right)&=3 x^{2} y' \cos{\left(y \right)} + 6 x \sin{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
6 x \sin{\left(y \right)} + y' \left(3 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} - 2\right)&=4 x\\
\left(3 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} - 2\right)y^{\prime}&=- 6 x \sin{\left(y \right)} + 4 x
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{- 6 x \sin{\left(y \right)} + 4 x}{3 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} - 2}
$$

This formula applies where $3 x^{2} \cos{\left(y \right)} - 2\ne0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(x^{2} \ln{\left(y \right)}\right)&=\frac{x^{2} y'}{y} + 2 x \ln{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2 x \ln{\left(y \right)} + y' \left(\frac{x^{2}}{y} + 3 y^{2}\right)&=6 x^{2}\\
\left(\frac{x^{2}}{y} + 3 y^{2}\right)y^{\prime}&=6 x^{2} - 2 x \ln{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{6 x^{2} - 2 x \ln{\left(y \right)}}{\frac{x^{2}}{y} + 3 y^{2}}
$$

This formula applies where $\frac{x^{2}}{y} + 3 y^{2}\ne0$. This relation requires $y>0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(2 x^{2} e^{y}\right)&=2 x^{2} y' e^{y} + 4 x e^{y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\prime}$, and factor:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x e^{y} + y' \left(2 x^{2} e^{y} - 6 y^{2}\right)&=4 x\\
\left(2 x^{2} e^{y} - 6 y^{2}\right)y^{\prime}&=- 4 x e^{y} + 4 x
\end{aligned}
$$

Divide by the coefficient of $y^{\prime}$:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}=\frac{- 4 x e^{y} + 4 x}{2 x^{2} e^{y} - 6 y^{2}}
$$

This formula applies where $2 x^{2} e^{y} - 6 y^{2}\ne0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\dfrac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.

